{
  "id": "celery_celery_10842",
  "repo_name": "celery/celery",
  "repo_url": "https://github.com/celery/celery",
  "base_commit": "a49e5092aacc7de8cf6fd94a6525bf2cfce49d09",
  "pr_number": 10842,
  "issue_number": 10841,
  "issue_title": "Prefork worker exits with \"RuntimeError: generator raised StopIteration\" after \"Process inqueue damaged\"",
  "issue_body": "## Summary\n\nWhen the prefork pool gives up writing a task to a child process's inqueue\n(more than 100 consecutive EAGAIN errors), the worker does not recover. Instead\nof terminating that child and rescheduling the task, it logs\n`Unrecoverable error: RuntimeError('generator raised StopIteration')`, shuts\ndown and exits with code 1.\n\n## Environment\n\n- celery 5.6.3, kombu 5.6.2, billiard 4.2.4, vine 5.1.0\n- prefork pool (default concurrency), worker started with embedded beat (`--beat`), Redis broker\n- Python 3.14.7, macOS 26.4 (arm64)\n- Still present on `main` at ed354d9100dbdaf7b867f103593b4dea4148670a\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nThe worker log shows:\n\n```\nERROR Process inqueue damaged: <ForkProcess(ForkPoolWorker-17\u2026)> BlockingIOError(35)\nCRITICAL Unrecoverable error: RuntimeError('generator raised StopIteration')\n```\n\nafter which the worker (and its embedded beat) shuts down and the process exits\nwith code 1. This happened 4 times in two days, in two separate deployments on\nthe same machine; each time the damaged child was the same pool slot\n(`ForkPoolWorker-17`).\n\nWhy the writes kept failing with EAGAIN is a separate question that this issue\ndoes not try to answer; it is about what happens after the pool gives up on the\nchild.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nThe pool terminates the damaged child, puts the job back so it is written to\nanother child, and the worker keeps running. That is what `on_not_recovering()`\nalready does; only the way the write generator ends afterwards is broken.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`AsynPool._create_write_handlers()` defines `_write_job()`, a generator that the\nevent loop advances each time the child's inqueue is writable. When writing the\nheader or the body fails with an errno in `UNAVAIL` more than 100 times in a\nrow, it calls `on_not_recovering()` and then does `raise StopIteration()`:\n\n- https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/ed354d9100dbdaf7b867f103593b4dea4148670a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py#L994-L996 (header)\n- https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/ed354d9100dbdaf7b867f103593b4dea4148670a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py#L1010-L1012 (body)\n\nSince PEP 479 (always on from Python 3.7), a `StopIteration` raised inside a\ngenerator body is converted to `RuntimeError('generator raised StopIteration')`\nwhen it leaves the generator. The callers that drive the generator\n(`Hub.create_loop()` in kombu, `schedule_writes()`, `AsynPool._flush_writer()`)\ncatch `StopIteration` as \"write finished\"; they do not catch `RuntimeError`.\n`Hub.create_loop()` removes the fd and re-raises it, it propagates out of\n`asynloop()` and the consumer, and `WorkController.start()` logs it as\n`Unrecoverable error` and stops the worker with exit code 1.\n\n`_write_ack()` in the same function has the same pattern in its\n\"process died, discard the ack\" branch (synack mode):\n\n- https://github.com/celery/celery/blob/ed354d9100dbdaf7b867f103593b4dea4148670a/celery/concurrency/asynpool.py#L1043-L1046\n\nThe fix is to `return` from the generator at these sites, which ends it with a\nplain `StopIteration` from `next()`, as the callers expect.\n\n## Impact\n\nA transient condition in one child process takes down the whole worker. Under\na supervisor without automatic restart, all background processing stops (tasks\nand, with `-B`, periodic tasks) until someone restarts the worker by hand.\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-10-12T16:46:42Z",
